---
import Legal from '../layouts/Legal.astro';
import { REPO } from '../lib/releases.js';

const CONTACT = 'qrate@dvnl.work';
---

<Legal
  title="Privacy policy"
  eyebrow="Privacy"
  updated="25 November 2026"
  description="qrate is a desktop app with no accounts or usage analytics. Learn what stays on your device, and what the optional Google Sheets sync and AI assistant send to other providers."
  lede="Your catalogue stays on your own disk unless you choose a feature that shares selected data with another service."
>
  <h2>The short version</h2>
  <p>
    The application has no accounts, no usage analytics and no automatic crash reporting. Your
    project stays on your device. Two optional features send project data to another provider, and
    both are off until you turn them on:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Google Sheets export and sync</strong> sends the rows and columns you export to Google,
      under your own Google account. See <a href="#google">Google Sheets</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The AI assistant</strong> sends your prompts, and the project information it reads to
      answer them, to an AI model provider: OpenRouter by default. See <a href="#assistant">AI assistant</a>.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Submitting feedback with details, or using a plugin, can also share information you choose with
    another service. qrate also checks release and plugin catalog metadata, and downloads components
    when you request them. Those network requests contain no project records. This website counts
    page views without cookies; that is the one difference between the website and the application.
  </p>

  <h2>What qrate collects</h2>
  <p>
    The application does not automatically send your catalogue, file paths, settings or usage
    data to the qrate team. It makes requests to qrate's release and plugin catalog services; those
    services receive ordinary connection information such as your IP address and user agent, but
    not your project contents. qrate writes a session log on your own machine to help you diagnose
    problems. When you open the feedback form, qrate gives your browser a short log preview. The
    form sends that preview to us only if you choose to include it.
  </p>

  <h2>This website</h2>
  <p>
    This site serves pages, images, and an optional feedback form, without accounts. Cloudflare serves
    it, and records standard request information for delivery and abuse prevention. That
    information includes your IP address, your user agent and the page you asked for. We do not use
    it to build profiles, and we run no advertising scripts. GitHub serves the application
    downloads. Their own privacy policy applies once you follow a download link.
  </p>
  <p>
    The site does run <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/web-analytics/" rel="noopener"
      >Cloudflare Web Analytics</a
    >, which counts page views so we can tell whether the download instructions are reaching
    anyone. It sets no cookie, stores no identifier in your browser, and assigns you no visitor ID
    that would follow you between sessions or to any other site. It reports the page you viewed,
    the referrer, and coarse details such as country, browser and device type. It has no access to
    your catalogue, and it runs only on this website. Cloudflare discards the IP address at the
    edge rather than storing it with the measurement. The feedback page does not load this analytics script.
    To opt out, block <code>static.cloudflareinsights.com</code>; any content
    blocker will do it, and the site works exactly the same without the script.
  </p>
  <p>
    One address on this site answers qrate rather than a browser. It hands the application its own
    Google API credentials, so we can rotate them without shipping a new release. It carries no
    personal data in either direction. Your Google sign-in never passes through it. That happens
    between your browser, Google, and your own computer.
  </p>

  <h2>Optional feedback reports</h2>
  <p>
    The Feedback button opens a browser form with app version, commit, operating system,
    architecture, project dimensions, plugin counts, and a short current-session log preview.
    qrate removes your home-directory path from the log preview. The URL fragment holds this data
    locally in your browser, where browser extensions can access it. The page removes the fragment
    before it loads third-party scripts. It lets you edit or exclude the summary and does not show
    the log preview until you choose to include it.
  </p>
  <p>
    Only submitting the form sends its contents to us. Reports can include your description,
    optional contact email, diagnostic summary, and files you explicitly attach.
    Inspect logs and screenshots for private information before attaching them.
    Cloudflare processes the submission and uses Turnstile and request information to prevent abuse.
    Linear stores reports and attachments for the qrate team. Reports are not public GitHub issues.
  </p>
  <p>
    We use reports to investigate issues, improve qrate, and reply if you provide an email.
    Reports remain in Linear while needed for investigation and follow-up.
    To request deletion, email <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT}`}>{CONTACT}</a> with your report ID.
    Provider backup and security retention policies may apply after deletion.
  </p>

  <p>
    The browser converter reads your .qrate file on your device. It sends no project data to us.
    If you choose Google Sheets export, the page asks you to sign in to Google and sends the
    selected rows and columns to Google for that export.
  </p>
  <h2 id="google">Google Sheets</h2>
  <p>
    qrate can export a project to a new Google Sheet, or sync it to a spreadsheet you already own.
    Both are optional and off by default. They appear only after you turn on Google Sheets in <strong>Settings ▸ Google</strong>, and nothing reaches Google until you start an export or sync
    and consent in your browser. Importing a public sheet by link does not use a Google account.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Scope requested:</strong> <code>drive.file</code> only. This scope reaches only the
      files qrate itself creates and the ones you hand it by name through Google's own file chooser.
      It gives qrate no access to the rest of your Drive: not your other documents, not your
      folders, not your file list.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Choosing an existing sheet:</strong> qrate opens Google's file chooser in your browser.
      Google shows you your own files and tells qrate about the one you pick, and nothing else. The
      chooser page on this site never sees the list. It passes your selection back to qrate on your
      own machine.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>What is sent:</strong> the rows, columns and cell notes of the project you export or
      sync, sent directly from your computer to Google's API. A sync replaces the contents of the
      chosen spreadsheet's first tab, and qrate asks you to confirm before it writes.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Where it goes:</strong> straight to Google. The data does not pass through any server
      operated by this project.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Sign-in:</strong> a loopback redirect with PKCE. qrate opens your browser, you consent
      to Google directly, and Google returns the result to a port on your own machine. qrate never
      sees your Google password.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Credentials:</strong> qrate keeps the refresh token Google issues in your operating
      system's credential store (Windows Credential Manager, macOS Keychain, or the Secret Service on
      Linux), so it does not ask for consent on every export. It never sends the token to us.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Retention and deletion:</strong> we hold no copy of your Google data, so there is
      nothing for us to delete. Turning Google Sheets off in <strong>Settings ▸ Google</strong>
      deletes the stored sign-in from your computer. You can also remove qrate's access from your
      Google account.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    You can withdraw consent at any time from your Google account's <a
      href="https://myaccount.google.com/permissions"
      rel="noopener">third-party access page</a
    >. Sheets qrate has already created or synced stay in your Drive and remain yours.
  </p>

  <h2>Limited Use</h2>
  <div class="callout">
    qrate's use and transfer of information received from Google APIs to any other app will adhere to
    the <a
      href="https://developers.google.com/terms/api-services-user-data-policy"
      rel="noopener">Google API Services User Data Policy</a
    >, including the Limited Use requirements.
  </div>
  <p>
    In practice that means Google user data is used only to provide the export you requested. It is
    not sold, not transferred to anyone, not used for advertising, and not read by any human.
  </p>

  <h2>Other things qrate can reach</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Release checks, the plugin catalog and components.</strong> qrate requests signed
      release and catalog metadata from <code>qrate.dvnl.work</code>. When you install a component,
      qrate downloads its files from the source listed for that component, such as GitHub or
      Hugging Face. These requests carry standard connection information required to serve the
      request, not your project data.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Dictionaries.</strong> If you add a spell-check language, qrate downloads that word
      list from its public repository. The request carries no identifying information beyond what any
      download requires.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Plugins.</strong> Plugins run from a folder you control. A plugin you install may
      contact a server you point it at, for example an Islandora site that checks vocabulary terms.
      That connection is yours, and that service's own policy governs it.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Preview tools.</strong> PDFium and ffmpeg run locally. They send nothing anywhere.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Visual search.</strong> Its model is downloaded from Hugging Face when you turn visual
      search on, and then runs entirely on your computer. No image or search text leaves it.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="assistant">AI assistant</h2>
  <p>
    qrate has an optional AI assistant: Pi, an open-source agent that runs on your computer in
    qrate's Agent panel. The base downloads, and the packages in the Microsoft Store, WinGet and
    Homebrew, do not include it. qrate downloads it from GitHub only when you choose to install it.
    The assistant does nothing until you open <strong>Agent ▸ Terminal</strong> and send it a
    message.
  </p>
  <p>
    The assistant itself runs on your device, but the AI model it talks to does not. To answer you,
    the assistant sends requests to an AI model provider over the internet.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Who processes it:</strong> by default <a href="https://openrouter.ai/privacy"
        rel="noopener">OpenRouter</a
      >, using its <code>openrouter/free</code> router, which passes each request on to one of the
      model providers it works with. If you choose another model or provider in Pi, requests go to
      that provider instead. OpenRouter and the model provider handle the data under their own
      privacy, retention and model-training terms. Some providers, particularly of free models, may
      keep prompts or use them to improve their models; OpenRouter lists each provider's policy and
      lets a signed-in account restrict which providers it uses.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>What is sent:</strong> your messages, the conversation so far, and whatever project
      information the assistant reads to answer you. That can include column names, cell values,
      notes, change history, the problems qrate found, image previews of linked files, and the
      contents of files outside the project that you allowed it to read. The requests go from your
      computer to the provider. They do not pass through qrate's servers, and we never see them.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Sign-in and credentials:</strong> you can sign in to OpenRouter in the assistant with <code>/login openrouter</code>. Pi keeps that credential in qrate's private Pi profile on your
      computer. If you already signed in to OpenRouter with your own copy of Pi, qrate can let the
      assistant use that sign-in for the session. It passes it to the assistant in memory, and never
      writes it to qrate's files or logs. No credential is ever sent to us.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>What stays on your computer:</strong> the conversation history of each project, Pi's
      settings and its sign-in, all in the <code>pi-agent</code> folder in qrate's data folder
      (<code>%LOCALAPPDATA%\qrate</code> on Windows, <code>~/Library/Application Support/qrate</code>
      on macOS, <code>~/.local/share/qrate</code> on Linux).
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Retention and deletion:</strong> delete the <code>pi-agent</code> folder to remove the
      conversations, settings and sign-in from your computer. Removing the assistant under <strong>Settings ▸ Components</strong> removes its program but leaves that folder. What a
      provider keeps is governed by its own terms; OpenRouter's covers deleting your OpenRouter
      account and its data.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>The assistant is limited to what you allow:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Every project starts in <strong>review</strong> mode, where the assistant can read the project
      and propose changes as drafts you accept or reject. You choose <strong>edit</strong> or <strong>connected</strong> mode yourself with <code>/qrate-mode</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      Changes it makes in edit mode are recorded in History, credited to you using the assistant,
      and can be undone. Deleting rows or columns always shows you its reason and waits for you to
      confirm.
    </li>
    <li>
      It reads a file or folder outside the project only after you approve that path. Approvals last
      until qrate closes, and you can revoke them at any time.
    </li>
    <li>
      In connected mode it can look terms up in public authority files (id.loc.gov, Wikidata and
      GeoNames), and only after you allow each one. Those lookups send the search terms, not your
      project.
    </li>
    <li>
      It asks before running any shell command or writing a file. On Linux and macOS, an approved
      command runs in a sandbox that sees only the project, a temporary folder and the files you
      allowed.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Other AI agents can read the open project through the <code>qrate</code> command-line tool, over
    a local connection protected by a token that is renewed each time qrate starts. Those agents are
    programs you run yourself, and their own providers' terms apply. Do not include sensitive
    information in a prompt unless you have reviewed the provider's terms.
  </p>

  <h2>Children</h2>
  <p>qrate is a professional cataloguing tool and is not directed at children under 13.</p>

  <h2>Changes</h2>
  <p>
    If this policy changes, the date at the top of this page changes with it, and the previous text
    stays in the site's <a href={`https://github.com/${REPO}`} rel="noopener">git history</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Contact</h2>
  <p>
    Questions about this policy, or about data qrate touches: <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT}`}
      >{CONTACT}</a
    >. See also the <a href="/terms">terms of service</a> and the <a href="/license"
      >license</a
    >.
  </p>
</Legal>
